= A1
{parent=Section A}
{c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=A1}

For the <gravitational-electrostatic balance of equal charged spheres>, the <shell theorem> and <Coulomb's law> give $F_g=GM^2/r^2$ and $F_e=q^2/(4\pi\epsilon_0r^2)$ for well-separated spherical particles. Their separation cancels at balance. Since $M=4\pi\rho a^3/3$,
$$
\boxed{a=\left(\frac{9q^2}{64\pi^3\epsilon_0G\rho^2}\right)^{1/6}
=\left[\frac{3|q|}{4\pi\rho\sqrt{4\pi\epsilon_0G}}\right]^{1/3}.}
$$
The two charges have the same sign, so electrostatic repulsion opposes gravitational attraction. A zero charge gives no nontrivial force balance. Spherical symmetry of the external charge distribution, or the leading far-field monopole approximation, is understood.

= A2
{parent=Section A}
{c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=A2}

By symmetry, the two grounded plates take equal induced surface charge and the <electric field> has magnitude $\sigma/(2\epsilon_0)$ between either plate and the sheet. The <grounded-plate potential of a central charged sheet> is therefore
$$
V_{\rm sheet}=\frac{\sigma d}{4\epsilon_0}\simeq282\,\mathrm V.
$$
An <electron> released at zero potential gains <kinetic energy> $eV_{\rm sheet}$. Thus
$$
\boxed{v=\sqrt{\frac{e\sigma d}{2\epsilon_0m_e}}\simeq9.97\times10^6\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}.}
$$
The planar infinite-sheet approximation neglects edge effects. Here $e>0$ denotes charge magnitude; the electron's negative charge makes it accelerate toward the positive sheet. Relativistic corrections are below0.1%. Numerical constants use rounded CODATA values, not a claim about the historical handbook: $\epsilon_0=8.85419\times10^{-12}\,\mathrm{F\,m^{-1}}$, $e=1.602176634\times10^{-19}\,\mathrm C$ and $m_e=9.10938\times10^{-31}\,\mathrm{kg}$. <CODATA recommended values of the fundamental physical constants: 2022 by National Institute of Standards and Technology>[Vacuum permittivity, elementary charge and electron mass]{ref}

= A3
{parent=Section A}
{c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=A3}

In the initial <centre-of-mass frame>, equal fragments acquire opposite velocities $\pm w$ by <conservation of momentum>. The released energy is
$$
\Delta K=2\left[\frac12\frac m2w^2\right]=\frac12mw^2.
$$
The <equal-mass fragmentation energy budget> needs $w=500\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ merely to match the other ship, costing $1.25\,\mathrm{GJ}$. To close an existing gap requires a little more than this. Using the full reserve gives
$$
\boxed{w_{\max}=\sqrt{\frac{2(2\times10^9)}{10^4}}=632\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}},\qquad
v_{\rm front}=2.632\,\mathrm{km\,s^{-1}}.}
$$
**The reserve is sufficient to overtake.** The rear half moves at $1.368\,\mathrm{km\,s^{-1}}$, preserving momentum. The kinetic-energy increase is the same in any inertial frame because total momentum is unchanged. Subsequent capture or braking would require additional interaction details; no target mass is needed for the interception test.

= A4
{parent=Section A}
{c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=A4}

The PDF's apex angle is $30^\circ$, so each contacted face makes $\alpha=15^\circ$ with the vertical. Let N be the inward <normal force> per face. Its vertical component is downward, $-N\sin\alpha$; limiting upward <static friction> contributes $\mu N\cos\alpha$. Symmetric contacts cancel horizontal forces and torque. Incipient lift consequently requires
$$
\boxed{N_{\min}=\frac{mg}{2(\mu\cos\alpha-\sin\alpha)}\simeq3.08\,\mathrm N\quad\text{per face}.}
$$
This is the usual **normal gripping force** convention, using $g=9.81\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$. Friction can lift the prism only if $\mu>\tan\alpha$, which is satisfied here.

If “force applied” means the magnitude of the entire finger-contact resultant, including its tangential component, the <normal versus resultant force in a frictional pinch> gives
$$
\boxed{F_{\min}=N_{\min}\sqrt{1+\mu^2}=3.31\,\mathrm N\quad\text{per face}.}
$$
The optimal resultant lies at the limiting <friction angle> $\arctan\mu$ from the inward normal. Stating which force is being measured avoids confusing these two legitimate quantities.

= A5
{parent=Section A}
{c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=A5}

For the <double-slit experiment> with oblique incidence, the <optical path difference> has an unknown incident contribution plus $a\sin\theta$ in the outgoing direction. It cancels between adjacent angular features. From a minimum to the next maximum, the path difference changes by $\lambda/2$:
$$
\boxed{\lambda=2a(\sin43^\circ-\sin17^\circ)
=4.68\times10^{-7}\,\mathrm m\simeq468\,\mathrm{nm}.}
$$
This <incidence-independent adjacent-fringe wavelength> uses the statement that intensity rises directly from the stated zero to the following peak. No absolute interference order or incidence angle is needed; nonadjacent extrema would require the number of intervening fringes.

= A6
{parent=Section A}
{c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=A6}

The central estimate is $c=4.15/1.93-1=1.15026$. With independent input standard errors, the <ratio-minus-one uncertainty> from first-order <uncertainty propagation> is
$$
s_c^2=\left(\frac1b\right)^2s_a^2+\left(\frac a{b^2}\right)^2s_b^2,
\qquad\boxed{c=1.150\pm0.063\quad\text{(standard error)}.}
$$
The four independent reported results cluster near1.21, within about one quoted standard error of this estimate, so **they are consistent at this precision**. Their tight clustering is not evidence that this measurement has the same precision. If the measurements of a and b were correlated, a covariance term $-2a\operatorname{Cov}(a,b)/b^3$ would be needed. <Guidelines for Evaluating and Expressing the Uncertainty of NIST Measurement Results by Barry N. Taylor and Chris E. Kuyatt>[Law of propagation of uncertainty and covariance contributions]{ref}

= Section B
{parent=Paper 1}
{c}
