= B7
{parent=Section B}
{c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=B7}

For a steady uniform jet, the mass flow rate is $\dot m=\rho Au$. The transported <momentum flux> and <kinetic energy flux of a uniform jet> are
$$
\boxed{\dot p=\rho Au^2,\qquad\dot K=\frac12\rho Au^3.}
$$
Incompressibility gives piston speed $v_p=Au/S$. Equating piston power $Fv_p$ to the jet's kinetic power gives
$$
\boxed{F=\frac12\rho Su^2.}
$$
This ideal result neglects losses and the kinetic energy of fluid moving slowly inside the large cylinder. Retaining an inlet speed correction would multiply the pressure expression by $1-(A/S)^2$.

A horizontal impact occurs at the projectile apex. Write $u_x=u\cos\theta$ and $u_y=u\sin\theta$. At its height h, $u_y^2=2gh$ and the flight time is $t=u_y/g$, so
$$
u_y=\sqrt{2gh},\qquad u_x=w\sqrt{\frac g{2h}},\qquad
\boxed{u=9.90\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}},\quad\theta=45^\circ,\quad F=4.91\,\mathrm N.}
$$
Here $g=9.81\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$ and $u_x=u_y=7.00\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$. The <apex-target condition for a ballistic jet> fixes both launch speed and angle.

If the target stops the horizontal momentum of the arriving water, the force is
$$
F_{\rm hit}=\dot m\,u_x=\rho A u u_x=0.277\,\mathrm N,
\qquad \mu m_tg=0.147\,\mathrm N.
$$
**This exceeds the limiting friction, so the target can be dislodged** in the ideal stopping-impact model. The <jet-area adjustment during ballistic slowing> matters: the nozzle area sets $\dot m$; replacing u by $u_x$ in that mass-flow factor would incorrectly assume unchanged jet area during deceleration. Reflection or a specified deflection would change the transferred momentum. Air drag, breakup and splash losses are excluded from the ballistic estimate.

\Image[paper-1-ia2011-jet-apex.png]
{title=Ballistic jet apex}
{source=paper-1-ia2011-jet-apex.py}

= i
{parent=B7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The net <force> equals the time derivative of <momentum> in an inertial frame:
$$
\boxed{\mathbf F_{\rm net}=\frac{d\mathbf p}{dt}.}
$$

= ii
{parent=B7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <work-energy theorem> gives $dK=\mathbf F_{\rm net}\cdot d\mathbf r$ for a point particle. For a <conservative force>, $\mathbf F=-\nabla U$ and its work is $-\Delta U$. Force is not generally a scalar derivative of total energy without specifying the displacement and the relevant energy.

= iii
{parent=B7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The instantaneous mechanical <power> supplied by a <force> is
$$
\boxed{P=\mathbf F\cdot\mathbf v,}
$$
where v is the velocity of its point of application in the chosen frame.

= iv
{parent=B7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

<Power> is the rate of <energy> transfer:
$$
\boxed{P=\frac{dE_{\rm transferred}}{dt}.}
$$
Net mechanical power equals the rate of kinetic-energy change in the work–energy description.

= B8
{parent=Section B}
{c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=B8}

The <moment of inertia> about an axis is $I=\int r_\perp^2\,dm$. The <parallel axis theorem> states $I=I_{\rm CM}+Ma^2$, where a is the perpendicular separation from a parallel axis through the centre of mass, and M is total mass.

Since $\rho=kr^2$, the density coefficient has units $\mathrm{kg\,m^{-5}}$. The <density-power cylindrical moment of inertia> follows from cylindrical shells:
$$
M=\int_0^R2\pi Lkr^3\,dr=\frac{\pi kLR^4}{2},\qquad
I_{\rm CM}=\int_0^R2\pi Lkr^5\,dr=\frac{\pi kLR^6}{3}
=\boxed{\frac23MR^2}.
$$
For an offset pivot, the <physical pendulum> equation is $(I_{\rm CM}+Ma^2)\ddot\theta=-Mga\sin\theta$. At small amplitude,
$$
\boxed{T=2\pi\sqrt{\frac{a^2+2R^2/3}{ga}}.}
$$
For the <minimum-period offset of a physical pendulum>, minimizing $T^2$ is equivalent to minimizing $a+2R^2/(3a)$. Its derivative vanishes at
$$
\boxed{a_{\min}=R\sqrt{2/3},\qquad
T_{\min}=2\pi\sqrt{\frac{2R\sqrt{2/3}}g}.}
$$
The second derivative is positive. The period diverges as $a^{-1/2}$ near zero and grows as $a^{1/2}$ for large offset, giving one minimum. This assumes the stated ideal pivot can pass inside the body; an externally constrained pivot would restrict the allowable a. <University Physics Volume 1 by William Moebs and others>[§15.4, physical-pendulum torque equation and period]{ref}

\Image[paper-1-ia2011-pendulum-offset.png]
{title=Pendulum pivot offset}
{source=paper-1-ia2011-pendulum-offset.py}

= B9
{parent=Section B}
{c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=B9}

For the stated standard configuration, the <Lorentz transformation> is
$$
\boxed{t'=\gamma(t-vx/c^2),\quad x'=\gamma(x-vt),\quad y'=y,\quad z'=z,\quad
\gamma=(1-v^2/c^2)^{-1/2}.}
$$
Differentiate along the moving object's worldline and divide $dx'$ by $dt'$ to obtain the <velocity-addition formula>:
$$
\boxed{u_x'=\frac{u_x-v}{1-u_xv/c^2}.}
$$
For a receding source, two emissions separated by proper time $1/f'$ occur $\gamma/f'$ apart in S. The second signal travels an additional distance $v\gamma/f'$, so its reception interval is $\gamma(1+v/c)/f'$. Hence the longitudinal <relativistic Doppler effect> is
$$
\boxed{f=\frac{f'}{\gamma(1+v/c)}=f'\sqrt{\frac{1-v/c}{1+v/c}}.}
$$
The inverse velocity transformation for the forward throw gives
$$
\boxed{u=\frac{c/2+c/7}{1+1/14}=\frac35c.}
$$
The ball approaches the stationary receiver, so its forward light is blueshifted. The received wavelength is $400\sqrt{(1-3/5)/(1+3/5)}=\boxed{200\,\mathrm{nm}}$, in the <ultraviolet>. **The stated monochromatic emission is outside the visible band.** Other reflected or emitted frequencies have not been specified. <University Physics Volume 3 by Samuel J. Ling and others>[§5.7, longitudinal light Doppler shift and approach/recession signs]{ref}

