= D12
{parent=Section D}
{c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=D12}

For a small-slope string element, the transverse net <tension> is $T[y_x(x+\delta x)-y_x(x)]\simeq Ty_{xx}\delta x$. Its mass is $\rho\delta x$, so <Newton's second law> gives
$$
\boxed{y_{tt}=\frac T\rho y_{xx},\qquad c^2=T/\rho.}
$$
Its extra length is $[\sqrt{1+y_x^2}-1]\delta x\simeq y_x^2\delta x/2$. Work against the approximately constant tension gives the <elastic energy of a small-slope string>, while transverse motion gives its kinetic energy:
$$
\boxed{dU=\frac T2y_x^2\,\delta x,\qquad dK=\frac\rho2y_t^2\,\delta x.}
$$
For a single travelling wave $y=f(x\pm ct)$, $y_t^2=c^2y_x^2$, so $dK=dU$ pointwise to this order. A standing wave need not have pointwise or instantaneous kinetic–potential equality.

For a fixed-end string of length L, taking the stated note to be its fundamental gives $c=2Lf$. Hence
$$
\boxed{T=\rho(2Lf)^2=16.5\,\mathrm N.}
$$
If98Hz were instead the nth harmonic, the required tension would be $16.5/n^2\,\mathrm N$; the usual fundamental interpretation is explicit.

For the specified standing wave, $\dot y=A\omega\sin kx\cos\omega t$. Every allowed nonzero fixed-end mode has $\int_0^L\sin^2kx\,dx=L/2$, yielding
$$
\boxed{K_{\max}=\frac14\rho LA^2\omega^2=4.25\times10^{-4}\,\mathrm J,\qquad\omega=2\pi(98\,\mathrm{Hz}).}
$$
At this instant the ideal mode's elastic energy is zero; at maximum displacement all its energy is elastic. <University Physics Volume 1 by William Moebs and others>[Chapter16, transverse-string wave speed, wave energy and fixed-end normal modes]{ref}

= D13
{parent=Section D}
{c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=D13}

In the time-independent <Schrödinger equation>, $-\hbar^2\psi''/(2m)$ is the kinetic-energy operator acting on the state, $V\psi$ its potential-energy contribution and $E\psi$ the energy-eigenvalue term. The complex <wave function> is a probability amplitude: the <Born rule> assigns density $|\psi|^2$ for a normalized single-particle state. A plane-wave beam uses an incident-amplitude or flux normalization instead of a unit-integral wavefunction.

For a free plane wave, $\psi''=-k^2\psi$, so $\boxed{E=\hbar^2k^2/(2m)}$ and its time factor obeys $\hbar\omega=E$. Above the upward <potential step>, the bounded subthreshold solution has decay constant
$$
\boxed{\alpha=\frac{\sqrt{2m(V_0-E)}}\hbar.}
$$
For the PDF's unit incident amplitude, write $\psi=e^{ikx}+Re^{-ikx}$ on the left and $\psi=Te^{-\alpha x}$ on the right, omitting their common time phase. Continuity of both $\psi$ and $\psi'$ at this finite step gives
$$
1+R=T,\qquad ik(1-R)=-\alpha T,\qquad
R=\frac{k-i\alpha}{k+i\alpha},\quad T=\frac{2k}{k+i\alpha}.
$$
Consequently
$$
\boxed{|R|^2=1,\qquad |T|^2=\frac{4k^2}{k^2+\alpha^2}=\frac{4E}{V_0}.}
$$
The <evanescent amplitude versus transmitted current> distinction matters: the second quantity is a squared amplitude, **not a transmitted-flux probability**. The decaying tail has zero <probability current>, despite nonzero density and the possible value $|T|^2>1$.

Using $\hbar=1.05457\times10^{-34}\,\mathrm{J\,s}$ and the electron mass and elementary charge specified above, the energy difference1eV gives $\alpha=5.12\,\mathrm{nm^{-1}}$. Thus at the stated position
$$
\boxed{|\psi_t(1\,\mathrm{nm})|^2=\frac83e^{-2(5.123)(1)}\simeq9.46\times10^{-5}.}
$$
This is relative to the unit incident density in the source figure, not an absolute normalized probability per metre. <CODATA recommended values of the fundamental physical constants: 2022 by National Institute of Standards and Technology>[Reduced Planck constant, electron mass and electron volt conversion]{ref}

\Image[paper-1-ia2011-step-density.png]
{title=Evanescent step density}
{source=paper-1-ia2011-step-density.py}

