= a
{parent=E14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

<Gauss's law> states
$$
\boxed{\oint_{\partial V}\mathbf E\cdot d\mathbf A=\frac{Q_{\rm enc}}{\epsilon_0},\qquad
\nabla\cdot\mathbf E=\rho_e/\epsilon_0.}
$$
The surface is closed and oriented outward, and $Q_{\rm enc}$ includes all enclosed charge. The integral law follows from the local form through the <divergence theorem>. It is universally valid in classical electromagnetism, but calculating the field directly from the flux requires enough symmetry.

For spherical charge symmetry, $E(r)=Q(<r)/(4\pi\epsilon_0r^2)$. A uniform infinite line gives $E=\lambda/(2\pi\epsilon_0r)$ from a coaxial cylinder. An infinite sheet gives $E=\sigma/(2\epsilon_0)$ on either side from a pillbox, with the direction set by charge sign. The normal field jump is $\sigma/\epsilon_0$; the electrostatic field inside a conductor is zero. Zero enclosed charge implies zero net flux, not generally zero field. <University Physics Volume 2 by Samuel J. Ling and others>[Chapter6, Gaussian surfaces and symmetry applications]{ref}

= b
{parent=E14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For a steady thin-wire current in vacuum, the <Biot-Savart law> is
$$
\boxed{\mathbf B(\mathbf r)=\frac{\mu_0I}{4\pi}\int\frac{d\boldsymbol\ell'\times(\mathbf r-\mathbf r')}{|\mathbf r-\mathbf r'|^3}.}
$$
The line element follows conventional current direction. More generally replace $I\,d\boldsymbol\ell'$ with $\mathbf J(\mathbf r')d^3r'$. The cross product gives the circulating field direction and superposition allows contributions from separate wire segments to be added.

Integration gives $B=\mu_0I/(2\pi r)$ around a long straight wire. On the axis of a circular loop of radius a, $B_z=\mu_0Ia^2/[2(a^2+z^2)^{3/2}]$, including the centre value $\mu_0I/(2a)$. Coil turns multiply the field when their geometries coincide; distributed coils and solenoids follow by integration. This is a magnetostatic or quasistatic calculation: rapidly changing currents require retarded electrodynamics and appropriate displacement-current effects, not an instantaneous wire formula. <University Physics Volume 2 by Samuel J. Ling and others>[Chapter12, steady-current fields and Biot–Savart applications]{ref}

= c
{parent=E14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For a stationary closed circuit, <Faraday's law of induction> is
$$
\boxed{\mathcal E=\oint\mathbf E\cdot d\boldsymbol\ell=-\frac{d\Phi_B}{dt},\qquad
\nabla\times\mathbf E=-\frac{\partial\mathbf B}{\partial t}.}
$$
The loop orientation and magnetic-flux normal obey the right-hand convention. For a moving material circuit, the emf is instead $\oint(\mathbf E+\mathbf v\times\mathbf B)\cdot d\boldsymbol\ell$, with the same negative derivative of the flux through the moving circuit. The additional magnetic force term accounts for motional emf.

<Lenz's law> expresses the minus sign physically: an induced current opposes the change in flux that produces it. It does not always oppose the field itself. Increasing positive flux drives a current producing negative flux; decreasing it reverses that response. A straight conducting rod of length l moving across a perpendicular uniform field has emf magnitude Blv. A resistive return circuit then develops a magnetic braking force, and the supplied mechanical power equals Joule heating in the ideal steady limit. This connects induction with <conservation of energy>. <University Physics Volume 2 by Samuel J. Ling and others>[Chapter13, Faraday induction, Lenz direction and motional emf]{ref}

= d
{parent=E14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

A <conservative force> has work depending only on the endpoints, so it can be written
$$
\boxed{\mathbf F=-\nabla U,\qquad W_{A\to B}=U(A)-U(B),\qquad
\oint\mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r=0.}
$$
The arbitrary additive constant in <potential energy> has no effect on force. A smooth curl-free force is conservative on a simply connected domain; a hole or excluded singularity requires checking global circulation as well.

Uniform gravity has $U=mgz$, central gravity has $U=-GMm/r$ with zero at infinity, and a linear spring has $U=kx^2/2$ relative to equilibrium. If the potential is time independent and no other force does work, $K+U$ is conserved by the <work-energy theorem>. A time-dependent potential or nonconservative work can change mechanical energy; having an instantaneous gradient force alone is not sufficient for energy conservation. <University Physics Volume 1 by William Moebs and others>[Chapter8, conservative forces, potential energy and mechanical-energy conservation]{ref}

= E15
{parent=Section E}
{c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=E15}

Treat Earth as a stationary spherical source, with $\mu=GM_E$, and neglect drag and other bodies. The conserved quantities are total mechanical energy and the <angular momentum> vector, implying a fixed orbital plane and constant areal velocity. The Kepler problem additionally conserves the <Laplace-Runge-Lenz vector>. The orbiting object's linear momentum is not constant because gravity exerts a force.

For a circular orbit, $mv_c^2/r_0=\mu m/r_0^2$, so $K=\mu m/(2r_0)=-U/2$. Hence
$$
\boxed{E_0=K+U=-\frac{\mu m}{2r_0}.}
$$
At the closest approach of a noncircular orbit, the velocity is tangential and $v_0=k\sqrt{\mu/r_0}$. Its energy and specific angular momentum are
$$
\boxed{E=\frac{\mu m}{2r_0}(k^2-2)=E_0(2-k^2),\qquad
h=r_0v_0=k\sqrt{\mu r_0}.}
$$
The <pericentre-speed qualification of Kepler orbit classes> requires $k\ge1$ when $r_0$ is genuinely the closest approach. For an ellipse, conservation of h gives $v_m=v_0r_0/r_m$. Equating energies at the two turning points and writing $x=r_m/r_0$ gives
$$
k^2-2=\frac{k^2}{x^2}-\frac2x,\qquad
(x-1)[(k^2-2)x+k^2]=0.
$$
The distinct apocentre root is therefore
$$
\boxed{\frac{r_m}{r_0}=\frac{k^2}{2-k^2},\qquad 1<k<\sqrt2.}
$$
For an unbound orbit, $v^2(r)=2E/m+2\mu/r$ decreases toward infinity. The <asymptotic minimum speed of an unbound Kepler orbit> is
$$
\boxed{v_\infty=\sqrt{\frac{\mu}{r_0}(k^2-2)}.}
$$
It is the limiting infimum, reached only asymptotically: positive for a hyperbola and zero for a parabola. The turning-point interpretation of $r_0$ is essential; if $0<k<1$, the same instantaneous energy describes a bound orbit whose $r_0$ is the apocentre instead. <University Physics Volume 1 by William Moebs and others>[Chapter13, gravitational energy, escape and Kepler orbits]{ref}

= i
{parent=E15}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The orbit is **circular for $\boxed{k=1}$**. The speed equals $\sqrt{GM_E/r_0}$ and radial acceleration balances the changing direction of the velocity.

= ii
{parent=E15}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

A noncircular **ellipse with $r_0$ at pericentre has $\boxed{1<k<\sqrt2}$**. Its energy is negative. Merely specifying $k<1$ would make the stated closest-approach point an apocentre, so it is excluded by the geometry; the circular case is listed separately.

= iii
{parent=E15}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The orbit is **unbound for $\boxed{k\ge\sqrt2}$**: parabolic at equality and hyperbolic above it. The pericentre speed has reached or exceeded the local <escape velocity>.
